the number of people in england  estimated to have coveted 19 has dropped  40 in just a week  look at the number of cases over the  past year after spiking  over the winter cases now mirror those  seen  before the pandemic and summer of last  year  a sage report found the vaccine might  fail to prevent  hospital admissions among a very small  number of people particularly  the elderly between december the 8th  when the uk began  its mass vaccination rollout and april  the 10th there were over  50 000 hospital admissions  three and a half thousand of those had  received the first dose  of a covet 19 vaccine  for most of these patients the time  between vaccination  and hospital admission was up to 15 days  where infection occurred before immunity  had developed  the researchers say there is a  possibility that vaccinated  elderly and vulnerable people may have  been exposed to coronavirus after  wrongly assuming  they were immune 526 people with a  positive covert  19 tests were admitted to hospital more  than three weeks  after receiving their first dose  113 died  these cases represent vaccine failure  which occurs when a person contracts a  disease  in spite of being vaccinated against it  but  experts say despite this there is still  a lot of cause  for optimism this is really good news  it's real world data  but the cautionary tale is it does take  about three weeks to get really good  immunity from the first  vaccine jab particularly for the older  population  so people do need to be careful for just  a little time after they've had that  first vaccination  and make sure they get the second job  later all the data  is positive and it's very encouraging  the vaccine rollout is having  a real impact on the virus there are  always  caveats in a pandemic but we can  certainly look forward to the summer  with increasing confidence
 

